  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 147 AM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nAs the mid-level trough across the Upper Midwest slides eastward, \nridging filters into the area along with a surface high moving south \nout of the Upper Midwest on Friday. The surface high is set to \narrive by Friday night, working to reinforce calm winds, drier low-\nlevel air, and a mostly clear sky across the area this weekend. This \nwill set the stage for efficient radiational cooling, allowing \ntemperatures to reach the 30s areawide both Friday and Saturday\nnights. The only exception being the St. Louis metro area that \nmay struggle to cool into the 30s thanks to the urban heat \nisland effect. Nearly all rural areas will at least approach the\nfreezing mark this weekend with many locations having a good \nchance (>50%) to fall below freezing either night. Confidence is\nhigh that river valleys and low-lying areas across the region, \nwhere cold air drainage is greatest, will see the first freeze \nof the season. With 850mb temps near 5C this weekend, highs are \nforecast to be in the 50s to low 60s each afternoon.\n\nBy next week, mid-level ridging should remain in place keeping the \narea dry through at least the first half of the week. In fact, \nguidance is in good agreement with less than a 10% chance for any \nrain until next Thursday. A gradual warming trend is expected and \nrevealed by LREF IQRs that become centered on values that slightly \nabove seasonal normals.",
